capability seal/aes-gcm  a text sealed and opened under an AES key of 128 or 256 bits in GCM, with associated data
  heroes  seal.seal, seal.open, seal.Algorithm
  go      aes.NewCipher, cipher.NewGCM, cipher.NewGCMWithRandomNonce, cipher.AEAD, cipher.AEAD.Seal, cipher.AEAD.Open
  rust    ring::aead::AES_128_GCM, ring::aead::AES_256_GCM, ring::aead::LessSafeKey, ring::aead::Aad
  python  AESGCM, AESGCM.encrypt, AESGCM.decrypt
  note    The system's own cipher on each platform: OpenSSL 3's `EVP_aes_*_gcm` on Linux (every NIST CAVP case of a 96-bit IV and a 128-bit tag, 1113 valid held and 387 FAIL refused, and Wycheproof's 133, run through the member's C probe on Linux arm64 2026-11-17), CNG's AES in GCM chaining on Windows (written from the Win32 reference, compiled against MinGW-w64's headers, UNRUN: the box was off). PENDING on macOS: CommonCrypto's public modes are ECB, CBC, CFB, CTR, OFB, RC4 and CFB8 (`CommonCryptor.h`, macOS 27.2 SDK), its GCM is in a private header the SDK does not ship, and CryptoKit, which has it, is Swift's alone; `is_available` answers false there and every call fails `unsupported`, never a cipher of Heroes' own. Sealed bytes are the nonce, the ciphertext and the tag, Go's `NewGCMWithRandomNonce` layout
capability seal/chacha20-poly1305  a text sealed and opened under ChaCha20-Poly1305 (RFC 8439), with associated data
  heroes  seal.seal, seal.open, seal.Algorithm
  go      -
  rust    ring::aead::CHACHA20_POLY1305
  python  ChaCha20Poly1305, ChaCha20Poly1305.encrypt, ChaCha20Poly1305.decrypt
  note    Go's is `golang.org/x/crypto/chacha20poly1305`, outside the standard library. RFC 8439 § 2.8.2's vector and Wycheproof's 316 cases held on Linux (`EVP_chacha20_poly1305`); CNG's CHACHA20_POLY1305 on Windows, documented from Windows 10, UNRUN; PENDING on macOS as AES-GCM is
capability seal/keys  a secret key drawn by the system or made of bytes, its size, and its end
  heroes  seal.generate, seal.from_secret, seal.key_size, seal.close, seal.Key
  go      -
  rust    ring::aead::UnboundKey, ring::aead::Algorithm
  python  AESGCM.generate_key, ChaCha20Poly1305.generate_key
  note    Go's key is a `[]byte` the program holds; here a key is a handle and its secret stays in C: a drawn key never enters the program's memory, one made of bytes is copied once and the language's lent copy overwritten, and `close` overwrites C's copy. `aes.KeySizeError` is the code `invalid`
capability seal/sizes  the bytes of a nonce, of a tag, and what sealing adds
  heroes  seal.NONCE_BYTES, seal.TAG_BYTES, seal.OVERHEAD
  go      cipher.AEAD.NonceSize, cipher.AEAD.Overhead, aes.BlockSize
  rust    ring::aead::NONCE_LEN, ring::aead::MAX_TAG_LEN
  python  -
capability seal/nonce-bound  a key that refuses to seal past the texts its random nonces allow
  heroes  seal.MAX_SEALS
  go      -
  rust    ring::aead::NonceSequence
  python  -
  note    2^32 texts under one key, NIST SP 800-38D § 8.3's bound for random 96-bit nonces, counted in C under the key table's lock; past it `seal` fails `limit`. Go and Python leave the bound to the program; ring's `NonceSequence` makes the program count
capability seal/caller-nonce  a text sealed with a nonce the caller chooses
  heroes  refused: a nonce used twice under one key gives both texts away and lets anyone forge, and an API that takes the nonce makes that mistake one line long; `seal` draws it, and `open` reads any sealed bytes whose nonce comes first, so text sealed by Go or Python with their own nonces opens here
  go      cipher.AEAD.Seal (with its nonce), cipher.NewGCMWithNonceSize
  rust    ring::aead::SealingKey, ring::aead::OpeningKey, ring::aead::BoundKey, ring::aead::Nonce, ring::aead::LessSafeKey::seal_in_place_append_tag
  python  AESGCM.encrypt (with its nonce), ChaCha20Poly1305.encrypt (with its nonce)
  note    the floor keeps `seal_nonce_for_tests` for the known-answer tests alone, named as such, as `tls` keeps its unchecked connection
capability seal/counted-nonces  nonces counted by the key, for protocols whose peer computes them (TLS, QUIC records)
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    ring::aead::NonceSequence, ring::aead::BoundKey
  python  -
  note    a counter the key holds, never the caller's, is the one safe shape of a nonce not drawn; no protocol of Rebel needs it yet, `tls` keeping its records in the system's library
capability seal/short-tags-and-nonces  GCM with a tag shorter than 16 bytes or a nonce other than 12
  heroes  refused: NIST SP 800-38D allows them for formats that need them and each weakens the tag or makes the nonce a hash, and no format of Rebel needs one
  go      cipher.NewGCMWithTagSize, cipher.NewGCMWithNonceSize
  rust    -
  python  -
capability seal/other-aeads  AES-GCM-SIV, AES-SIV, AES-OCB3, AES-CCM and XChaCha20-Poly1305
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  AESGCMSIV, AESSIV, AESOCB3, AESCCM
  note    measured 2026-11-17: macOS offers none to C; CNG documents CCM as a chaining mode of AES; OpenSSL 3 has GCM-SIV, SIV, OCB and CCM. Each waits for a program that needs it, the face's `Algorithm` taking a case per cipher
capability seal/unauthenticated-modes  a block cipher in CBC, CFB, CTR or OFB, and streams of it
  heroes  refused: a mode with no tag lets anyone change the text unseen, and every such mode is the setting of a published attack (padding oracles, bit flipping); Rebel offers authenticated encryption alone
  go      cipher.NewCBCEncrypter, cipher.NewCBCDecrypter, cipher.NewCFBEncrypter, cipher.NewCFBDecrypter, cipher.NewCTR, cipher.NewOFB, cipher.BlockMode, cipher.Stream, cipher.StreamReader, cipher.StreamReader.Read, cipher.StreamWriter, cipher.StreamWriter.Write, cipher.StreamWriter.Close
  rust    -
  python  -
capability seal/raw-block  the AES block function alone
  heroes  refused: a block cipher alone is the part modes are built of, and building one is writing a cipher, which this package exists not to do
  go      aes.NewCipher (as a cipher.Block), cipher.Block
  rust    -
  python  -
capability seal/algorithm-names  an algorithm named as text and read back
  heroes  seal.format_algorithm, seal.parse_algorithm
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
capability seal/availability  whether this machine seals with an algorithm, asked before a key is made
  heroes  seal.is_available
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    false on macOS for every algorithm (`seal/aes-gcm`), and on a Linux machine with no `libcrypto.so.3`, which the member opens at its first call
capability seal/protocol-keys  ciphers shaped for one protocol: QUIC's header protection, OpenSSH's ChaCha20-Poly1305
  heroes  refused: each is that protocol's own construction, and belongs to a package of that protocol over the system's own cipher
  go      -
  rust    ring::aead::quic::HeaderProtectionKey, ring::aead::quic::Algorithm, ring::aead::quic::AES_128, ring::aead::quic::AES_256, ring::aead::quic::CHACHA20, ring::aead::chacha20_poly1305_openssh::SealingKey, ring::aead::chacha20_poly1305_openssh::OpeningKey, ring::aead::chacha20_poly1305_openssh::KEY_LEN, ring::aead::chacha20_poly1305_openssh::PACKET_LENGTH_LEN, ring::aead::chacha20_poly1305_openssh::TAG_LEN
  python  -

dropped in-place  sealing into the caller's own buffer (Python's `encrypt_into`, `decrypt_into`; ring's `seal_in_place_*`, `open_in_place`, `Tag`): every value is a copy, and `seal` answers a new list, the text's own cell overwritten
  go      -
  rust    ring::aead::Tag
  python  AESGCM.encrypt_into, AESGCM.decrypt_into

dropped interfaces  Go's `cipher.AEAD` as an interface and ring's `BoundKey` trait: there are no interfaces, and `Key` holds which algorithm it is
  go      cipher.AEAD
  rust    ring::aead::BoundKey
  python  -

dropped internals  Python's module plumbing
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  annotations, rust_openssl
